{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-32919",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The point $P(1,2,2)$ is at the tip of $r(t)$ for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t.$ At this point, the vector ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to $P.$ We know that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =i+2\\,j+2\\,k \\\\ t\\,i+2\\sqrt{t}\\,j+\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+3}\\,k & =i+2\\,j+2\\,k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand equating the coefficients of $i,j,$ and $k$ yields the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}t=1 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{t}=2 \\\\ \\sqrt{{t}^{2}+3}=2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $t=1.$ This value also satifies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(1,2,2)$ is at the tip of the vector $r(1).$  \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{t})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+3})\\,k \\\\  & =\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}\\,j+\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+3}}\\,k \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(1)}}\\,j+\\frac{(1)}{\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+3}}\\,k \\\\  & =\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the curve at the point $P(1,2,2)$ is $\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k.$",
